Consider the following function:

```c++
MyClass createObject() {
  MyClass obj; // Object created on the stack
  // ... some operations on obj ...
  return obj; // RVO/NRVO might be applied here
}
```

If RVO/NRVO is applied, the compiler will skip the creation of a temporary `MyClass` object on the stack within `createObject()` and instead directly construct the object into the memory space allocated for the return value by the caller.  The compiler's ability to perform these optimizations often relies on the object being returned by value and the function having a single return statement.

`std::move` can help enable RVO/NRVO in more complex scenarios but isn't always necessary as long as the compiler is able to understand the context and perform the optimization.
